<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Inalids</h1><p class="st-meta">1095 CE – 1183 CE · capital Amid</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkmen dynasty that ruled the walled city of Amid (Diyarbakir) for most of the 12th century after the fall of the Marwanids.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwanids">Marwanid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> in the 1080s, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> commander named Inal established himself in Amid in the 1090s, founding a small hereditary emirate on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Inalids held little more than Amid and its district and relied on alliances with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/artuqids-of-mardin">Artuqids</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zengid-dynasty">Zengids</a>. For long periods real power lay with their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> of the Nisanid family, local notables who managed the city. Inscriptions on the great basalt walls of Amid record building work in this period. The population included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac-christians">Syriac Christians</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1183 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> besieged and took Amid and handed it to his ally, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> Muhammad, merging it into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/artuqids-of-hasankeyf">Artuqids of Hasankeyf</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1095 CE – 1183 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.49%;width:1.75%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1095 CE – 1183 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amid</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Inal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkic, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Inalid dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1139&amp;focus=inalids" aria-label="Inalids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5683 815 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Inalids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6003 1071L6481 1127M6003 1071L6034 1077"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6481 1127h0M6034 1077h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6003 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6003 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1139&amp;focus=inalids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

